WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.027
When two factors change, the product has two first-order contributions

00:00:05.027 --> 00:00:13.357
One changes the first factor while retaining the second; the other changes the second while retaining the first

00:00:13.357 --> 00:00:16.133
Multiplying derivatives alone misses both contributions.

00:00:17.433 --> 00:00:25.804
For x squared times x plus three, the product rule gives two x times x plus three, plus x squared

00:00:25.804 --> 00:00:28.315
Now distribute and collect like terms

00:00:28.315 --> 00:00:32.500
The calculus rule comes first; ordinary algebra simplifies its result.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:40.805
For a quotient, differentiate the top times the bottom, then subtract the top times the differentiated bottom

00:00:40.805 --> 00:00:43.277
Divide by the original bottom squared

00:00:43.277 --> 00:00:48.633
Here the numerator becomes x plus four minus x minus one, leaving three.

00:00:49.933 --> 00:00:54.026
Sometimes factoring first is easier than using the quotient rule

00:00:54.026 --> 00:01:01.393
This expression equals x plus four except at four, so its derivative is one on its original domain

00:01:01.393 --> 00:01:04.667
The cancelled factor still leaves an excluded input.
